-- Bilateral settlement netting - turning a pile of small payments
-- between two parties into a single net payment, with the rules that
-- make that safe enforced by the runtime rather than by a spreadsheet.
--
-- A quick tour of the domain, in case settlement is not your world.
-- When two firms trade with each other all day, they end up owing each
-- other lots of small amounts in both directions: you owe me for this
-- trade, I owe you for that one. Settling every one of those separately
-- means a flurry of payments, each with its own cost and its own chance
-- of going wrong. So instead they "net": they add up everything one
-- owes the other, cancel the offsetting amounts, and replace the whole
-- pile with one payment for the difference. Each underlying obligation
-- here is a "settlement line" with a signed amount - positive means one
-- party receives, negative means it pays. The single combined payment
-- is the "net settlement".
--
-- Netting is only safe if a few things are true at once. Every line you
-- fold in must already be approved. The net figure must be exactly the
-- signed sum of those lines - not "close", exactly, or the books no
-- longer tie out. And no line may be swept into two different nets, or
-- it would be paid twice. Conventional systems check these rules in
-- application code, then run a reconciliation job the next morning to
-- check them again. Morpholog makes the rules the law of admission: a
-- netting that breaks any of them cannot enter the system's state at all.
--
-- Here is how Morpholog thinks about this. It does not store "facts" -
-- it stores *claims* it has agreed to admit because they obey the rules.
-- Two kinds of rule do the work below:
--
--   * an `invariant` is a rule that must hold for all admitted state,
--     forever. The runtime refuses to commit any change that would
--     break one - so the "net equals the sum of its lines" rule can
--     never be quietly false, even for an instant.
--   * a `require` is a gate on a single action, checked at the moment
--     you act and never again. The distinction matters here: "the lines
--     must be approved and not already netted" is a gate on the act of
--     netting - it is the right question to ask when someone proposes a
--     net, but it is not a property the world must preserve forever. An
--     invariant governs what is true; a gate governs what you may do next.

program settlement_netting

-- The vocabulary. Each `predicate` names a kind of claim this world can
-- hold and the shape of its arguments. These are the only nouns the
-- system knows: the net settlement itself, the lines folded into it, who
-- approved which line, which two parties a line sits between, what each
-- line is worth, and whether a line has already been netted.
predicate NetSettlement(net_id: Subject, party_a: Subject, party_b: Subject, amount: Decimal)
predicate SettlementLine(line_id: Subject, net_id: Subject, amount: Decimal)
predicate ApprovedSettlementLine(line_id: Subject)
predicate Between(line_id: Subject, party_a: Subject, party_b: Subject)
predicate LineAmount(line_id: Subject, amount: Decimal)
predicate Netted(line_id: Subject)

-- An intent is a message the system sends to the outside world after a
-- change commits - here, "a net settlement was created". It is how the
-- netting engine tells the rest of the business that the combined
-- payment now exists and can be acted on.
intent NetSettlementCreated(net: Subject)

-- The rules that must always hold. If any change below would leave the
-- world violating one of these, the whole change is rejected.

-- A net settlement that folds in no lines is meaningless: it would be a
-- payment for nothing. Every net must reference at least one line. Read
-- `implies` as "whenever the left is true, the right must be too".
invariant net_settlement_has_lines:
    NetSettlement(net, _, _, _) implies (exists line: SettlementLine(line, net, _))

-- The heart of netting: the net amount must equal the signed sum of its
-- lines, exactly. If the arithmetic does not tie out, the books are
-- wrong, and the runtime simply will not admit the net.
invariant net_amount_equals_lines:
    NetSettlement(net, _, _, amount) implies amount = sum(x | SettlementLine(_, net, x))

-- No line may belong to two different nets at once - that would settle
-- the same obligation twice. This is what makes "no double netting" a
-- property of the world rather than just a check at netting time.
invariant no_double_netting:
    SettlementLine(line, net, _) implies not (exists other: SettlementLine(line, other, _) and other != net)

-- The only way state may change here. A transformation proposes a set
-- of changes; the runtime applies them only if every invariant still
-- holds once they are in place. `admit` adds a claim.
--
-- Create a net settlement from a set of lines between two parties. The
-- gate runs first: every line must be approved, must sit between these
-- two parties, and must not already be netted - checked once, at the
-- moment of netting. Then we mint a fresh identifier for the net, total
-- up the lines, record the net and each line's membership in it, and
-- mark every line as netted so it can never be swept into a second net.
-- Notice we never check the total against the invariant by hand: the
-- "net equals the sum of its lines" invariant guarantees it at commit,
-- and any mismatch is simply refused.
transformation create_net_settlement(party_a, party_b, lines):
    require forall line in lines: ApprovedSettlementLine(line) and Between(line, party_a, party_b) and not Netted(line)

    let net = new Subject()

    let amount = sum(x | line in lines and LineAmount(line, x))

    admit NetSettlement(net, party_a, party_b, amount)

    for line in lines:
        bind LineAmount(line, amt)
        admit SettlementLine(line, net, amt)
        admit Netted(line)

    emit NetSettlementCreated(net)
